# Copy to .env and fill in real values. .env is gitignored.
#
# Already have a .env? Run `make check-env` after pulling: it lists the
# settings added here since that .env was created. An absent variable is
# interpolated into the stack as an empty string and deployed without
# comment, so the failure surfaces as a container dying on its own config
# (e.g. oauth2-proxy: "missing setting: cookie-secret") rather than as
# anything naming .env.

# --- Postgres superuser / default DB ---
POSTGRES_USER=postgres
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me
POSTGRES_DB=postgres

# --- pgAdmin ---
PGADMIN_EMAIL=admin@infra.famillelallier.net
PGADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me

# --- Keycloak ---
# Bootstrap admin account, created only on first boot against an empty
# Keycloak database (same first-run-only caveat as PGADMIN_EMAIL/PASSWORD
# above). Changing these later does not change an already-created admin.
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN=admin
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me

# --- Per-app databases ---
# Comma-separated list of app names. For each name "foo", a database "foo"
# and a role "foo" are created, owned by that role. Requires a matching
# FOO_DB_PASSWORD variable (uppercased app name) below.
APP_DATABASES=jarvis,nurse,keycloak,grafana,ea,airflow

JARVIS_DB_PASSWORD=change-me
NURSE_DB_PASSWORD=change-me
KEYCLOAK_DB_PASSWORD=change-me
GRAFANA_DB_PASSWORD=change-me
EA_DB_PASSWORD=change-me
# Embedded in Airflow's SQLAlchemy URL: letters/digits only.
AIRFLOW_DB_PASSWORD=change-me

# Read-only role for postgres-exporter (pg_monitor). Created on first boot
# by postgres/initdb/20-monitoring-role.sh; see README for existing clusters.
MONITORING_DB_PASSWORD=change-me

# --- Grafana ---
GRAFANA_ADMIN_USER=admin
GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me

# --- MinIO ---
# Root credentials for the object store (API + console). Password must be
# at least 8 characters. Created on first boot against an empty minio-data
# volume — changing these later does not rotate an already-initialized root.
MINIO_ROOT_USER=minioadmin
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=change-me

# Secret key for the MinIO user "obsidian" (access key = obsidian), which
# the obsidian service's Remotely Save plugin uses to sync vaults into the
# "obsidian" bucket. At least 8 characters. Applied by
# `make obsidian-minio`, not at boot — re-run it after changing this.
OBSIDIAN_MINIO_SECRET_KEY=change-me

# Secret key for the MinIO user "ea-api" (access key = ea-api), which the EA
# API stores its files with, in the "ea-catalogue" bucket. At least 8
# characters. Applied by `make ea-minio` -- re-run it after changing this, and
# copy it to EA_S3_SECRET_KEY in ~/OpenCode/EA/deploy/ea.env.
EA_MINIO_SECRET_KEY=change-me

# --- RabbitMQ ---
# Default user for AMQP + the management UI. Created on first boot against
# an empty rabbitmq-data volume — changing these later does not rotate an
# already-initialized user (use the management UI or rabbitmqctl instead).
RABBITMQ_DEFAULT_USER=infra
RABBITMQ_DEFAULT_PASS=change-me

# --- Jarvis app: Keycloak login gate (oauth2-proxy) ---
# jarvis-realm.json intentionally omits a client secret (Keycloak
# auto-generates one for the confidential "jarvis" client on first
# import) and does not create the login user itself. Two manual steps in
# the Keycloak admin console (https://keycloak.famillelallier.net) are
# required after the first `make up`:
#   1. Clients -> jarvis -> Credentials tab -> copy (or regenerate) the
#      "Client secret" value into JARVIS_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET below, then
#      `docker compose up -d oauth2-proxy` to pick it up.
#   2. Users -> Add user (realm "jarvis") -> set a password on the
#      Credentials tab. This is the one homelab user who will log in to
#      Jarvis; no password is or should be committed to this repo.
JARVIS_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=change-me

# Encryption key for oauth2-proxy's session cookie. Generate your own —
# do not reuse the example below or commit a real value:
#   openssl rand -base64 32 | tr -- '+/' '-_'
# The `tr` is not optional. oauth2-proxy decodes this with the *URL-safe*
# base64 alphabet only, and falls back to the raw string when that fails --
# so a plain `openssl rand -base64 32` (which emits a '+' or '/' about three
# times in four) arrives as a 44-byte key and the container dies with
# "cookie_secret must be 16, 24, or 32 bytes ... but is 44 bytes".
JARVIS_OAUTH_COOKIE_SECRET=change-me

# --- Obsidian: Keycloak login gate (oauth2-proxy, realm "ea") ---
# obsidian.infra.famillelallier.net is gated by its own oauth2-proxy
# (service oauth2-proxy-ea), against the "ea" realm rather than "jarvis":
# whoever may log in to EA may open Obsidian. Like the jarvis client,
# ea-realm.json's "ea-obsidian" client carries no secret -- Keycloak
# generates one on import -- so after the first `make up`:
#   1. Clients -> ea-obsidian -> Credentials tab -> copy the "Client secret"
#      into EA_OBSIDIAN_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET below, then
#      `docker compose up -d oauth2-proxy-ea` to pick it up.
#   2. Users in the "ea" realm need an email address: oauth2-proxy reads the
#      `email` claim and refuses a login without one.
EA_OBSIDIAN_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=change-me

# Encryption key for that proxy's session cookie -- its own, not shared with
# JARVIS_OAUTH_COOKIE_SECRET. Generate your own, base64url like the one
# above (see the note there for why the `tr` matters):
#   openssl rand -base64 32 | tr -- '+/' '-_'
EA_OBSIDIAN_OAUTH_COOKIE_SECRET=change-me

# --- Nurse app: Keycloak realm "nurse" seed-user passwords ---
# Login passwords for the demo nurse.demo / examiner.demo accounts defined
# in keycloak/realm-import/nurse-realm.json. Set by
# `make keycloak-seed-users` (scripts/keycloak-seed-users.sh) once the
# keycloak service is up.
NURSE_SEED_PASSWORD=change-me
EXAMINER_SEED_PASSWORD=change-me

# --- Neo4j (the EA repo's architecture graph) ---
# Read only on first boot against an empty neo4j-data volume.
NEO4J_PASSWORD=change-me

# --- Airflow (https://airflow.infra.famillelallier.net) ---
# Admin login, created only on the first deploy against an empty airflow
# database (same first-run-only caveat as PGADMIN_PASSWORD). An existing
# cluster needs the database first: `make provision-app app=airflow`.
AIRFLOW_ADMIN_USER=admin
AIRFLOW_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me
# Encrypts connections/variables at rest -- changing it later makes them
# unreadable. Fernet wants urlsafe base64 of 32 bytes:
#   openssl rand -base64 32 | tr -- '+/' '-_'
AIRFLOW_FERNET_KEY=change-me
# Shared by every airflow-* container to sign execution-API tokens:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
AIRFLOW_JWT_SECRET=change-me

# --- DNS (LAN-wide resolver for *.famillelallier.net, via Technitium) ---
# This Mac's own LAN IP. Docker Desktop publishes container ports on the
# host, so ports: ["${LAN_IP}:53:53/udp", ...] on the dns service binds this
# address on the Mac itself -- and it is also the answer the dns container
# hands out for infra.famillelallier.net / pgadmin.famillelallier.net /
# keycloak.famillelallier.net. Get a static DHCP reservation for this Mac
# from your router so it doesn't change. Find the current value with
# `ipconfig getifaddr en1` (Wi-Fi; en0 if you are on Ethernet).
#
# This used to be the Colima VM's own bridged address rather than the Mac's
# -- see "Runtime: Docker Desktop" in CLAUDE.md if you are following older
# notes.
#
# The stack now runs on the Windows laptop (laptop-dopbpc0j): this is its
# address, and the Makefile's INFRA_HOST must match it.
LAN_IP=192.168.2.10

# Technitium web console ("http://<LAN_IP>:5380") admin password.
DNS_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me

# Upstream resolvers for every query that isn't a famillelallier.net
# name this stack owns. Defaults to Cloudflare if left unset.
UPSTREAM_DNS=1.1.1.1
UPSTREAM_DNS_2=1.0.0.1

# --- Portainer API (make up / down / pull / clean) ---
# Lives in .portainer.env (gitignored), not here: this file is handed to
# containers via env_file (see postgres in docker-compose.yml), and
# PORTAINER_API_KEY is a Docker-daemon-root token that must never land in
# one. After 'make portainer-up' and creating the admin account, create a
# token under Portainer -> My account -> Access tokens and put it in
# .portainer.env:
#   PORTAINER_API_KEY=change-me
#   PORTAINER_ENDPOINT_ID=1
# (the second line is optional; it defaults to the local Docker environment)

# --- CI runner (make runner-up; deploy on a push to main) ---
# Lives in .runner.env (gitignored), not here, for the same reason
# PORTAINER_API_KEY does: this file is handed to containers via env_file and
# shipped to Portainer as the stack env, and GH_RUNNER_TOKEN is a GitHub PAT
# that may register runners on this repo. Create one under GitHub -> Settings
# -> Developer settings -> Personal access tokens (classic 'repo' scope, or a
# fine-grained token with Administration: read and write on this repo), then:
#   GH_RUNNER_TOKEN=change-me
#   GH_RUNNER_NAME=infra-host
# (the second line is optional; it names the runner in GitHub's UI)

# --- OpenBao / the vault (make vault-init / vault-seed / vault-env) ---
# Nothing for the vault belongs in this file. Its two credentials live
# elsewhere, for two different reasons:
#
#   openbao/seal.key   32 random bytes that let the vault unseal itself
#                      after a restart, and the only thing that can decrypt
#                      the openbao-data volume. Generated by `make seal-key`
#                      (which `make init` runs), gitignored, bind-mounted
#                      into the container as a file. Back it up off this
#                      machine -- lose it and the vault is unrecoverable.
#
#   .openbao.env       the root token and the break-glass recovery key,
#                      written by `make vault-init`:
#                        BAO_TOKEN=change-me
#                        OPENBAO_RECOVERY_KEY=change-me
#                      Gitignored, like .portainer.env and for the same
#                      reason: this file is handed to containers via
#                      env_file (see postgres in docker-compose.yml) and
#                      shipped to Portainer as the stack env, and a root
#                      token for the secret store must reach neither.
#
# Once the vault is seeded (`make vault-seed`), it -- not this file -- is
# where a secret gets edited; `make vault-env` regenerates .env from it.
